---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: LicenseRef-PolyForm-Noncommercial-1.0.0
import '../styles/site.css';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import NotifyForm from '../components/NotifyForm.astro';
---
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <title>Kirby — a HERO System virtual tabletop</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="A HERO System virtual tabletop whose numbers are provably right."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <section id="hero">
        <h1>A HERO System virtual tabletop whose numbers are provably right.</h1>
        <p>
          Kirby plays HERO 6E characters with a cost and combat engine that is
          checked, object by object, against Hero Designer itself &mdash; not
          asserted, measured.
        </p>
        <NotifyForm />
      </section>

      <section id="why">
        <h2>Why this exists</h2>
        <p>
          Hero Designer gets the maths right. That is the whole reason it is
          the reference: thirty years of HERO System rules, encoded and
          trusted at thousands of tables. Anything that wants to sit beside a
          character HD produced has to meet that standard, and the only
          honest way to know whether it does is to check every number against
          it.
        </p>
        <p>
          So we did, and it found our own bugs. An early version of the
          costing engine truncated 44.5 to 44 &mdash; it printed
          &ldquo;44&rdquo; on a sheet right beside text reading &ldquo;(45
          Active Points)&rdquo;. Nobody catches that in review. You catch it
          mid-session, when a player asks why their number doesn't match and
          the table stops to work out which one is wrong. Checking against
          Hero Designer, object by object, is what surfaced it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Foundry VTT already has good HERO systems, built by people who know
          the game. Kirby isn't trying to out-Foundry Foundry &mdash; it's a
          different bet: an engine whose every cost and combat number is
          validated against Hero Designer, so a character means the same thing
          in both.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="today">
        <h2>What works today</h2>
        <p>Three small, independent engines, each installable on its own.</p>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="https://github.com/pdbethke/kirby-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer">kirby-cost</a></h3>
          <p>
            <code>pip install kirby-cost</code>
          </p>
          <p>
            Reads a HERO 6E build and costs it. Validated against Hero
            Designer: 655 of 655 characters match, every object and every
            character total.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="https://github.com/pdbethke/kirby-sheet" rel="noopener noreferrer">kirby-sheet</a></h3>
          <p>
            <code>pip install kirby-sheet</code>
          </p>
          <p>
            Reads a <code>.hdc</code> and writes JSON, plain text, HTML, PDF,
            or a <code>.hdc</code> back out. PDF needs the optional
            <code>pdf</code> extra: <code>pip install "kirby-sheet[pdf]"</code>.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="https://github.com/pdbethke/kirby-combat" rel="noopener noreferrer">kirby-combat</a></h3>
          <p>
            <code>pip install kirby-combat</code>
          </p>
          <p>
            A pure-Python HERO 6E combat engine. Zero runtime dependencies,
            905 tests.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section id="yours">
        <h2>Yours to keep</h2>
        <p>
          Kirby reads a <code>.hdc</code> and writes a <code>.hdc</code> back
          out unchanged in everything it doesn't touch. Nothing about your
          characters is rewritten into a proprietary format you can't get
          back out.
        </p>
        <p>
          Self-hostable, and source-available, free for personal use, under
          PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0. Read the code, run it on your own
          machine, keep your own copy of your own characters.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="needs">
        <h2>What you still need</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>
            Hero Designer and your own rulebooks are required for kirby-cost
            and kirby-sheet.
          </strong>
          Kirby ships no templates, no rules text and no character data.
          Reading or writing a <code>.hdc</code> will not start without a
          <code>.hdt</code> from a real Hero Designer installation &mdash;
          point it at your own, and it exits with an error telling you so if
          you haven't. kirby-combat is the exception: it takes plain Python
          objects and needs no Hero Designer installation of its own &mdash;
          the numbers you hand it still have to come from your own
          rulebooks.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="roadmap">
        <h2>Roadmap, honestly</h2>
        <p>
          Kirby plays characters; it does not create them. Character creation
          stays in Hero Designer, on purpose &mdash; that's the tool built for
          it, and duplicating it isn't the goal here.
        </p>
        <p>
          The three engines above are the whole of what's built. The table
          experience they're meant to serve &mdash; a running combat tracker
          in front of players, not just a library underneath one &mdash; is
          not built yet. That's next, and it's the reason for the notify
          form above: there's nothing to demo before there's something worth
          demoing.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
